<article class="env-lemma" id="08ZE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08ZE">Lemma <span data-tag="08ZE">5.3.1</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a topological space. The following are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$X$ is Hausdorff, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the diagonal $\Delta (X) \subset X \times X$ is closed. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We suppose that $X$ is Hausdorff. Let $(x, y) \not\in \Delta (X)$, i.e., $x \neq y$. There are $U$ and $V$ disjoint open sets of $X$ such that $x \in U$ and $y \in V$. This implies that $U \times V \subset (X \times X) \setminus \Delta (X) $. This shows that $ (X \times X) \setminus \Delta (X)$ is an open set of $ X \times X$ which is equivalent to saying that the diagonal $\Delta (X) \subset X \times X$ is closed in $X \times X$. The converse is similar: The complement $(X \times X) \setminus \Delta (X)$ consists precisely of $(x, y) \in X\times X$ with $ x \neq y$. Thus, if $ \Delta (X) \subset X \times X$ is closed, then, by the definition of the product topology, for every such $ (x, y)$, there are opens $U, V \subset X$ with $(x, y) \in U \times V$ and $(U \times V)\cap \Delta (X) = \emptyset $. In other words, with $x \in U$ and $y \in V$ such that $U \cap V = \emptyset $. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>